// Fixture: delete_multi_generation_chain
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4 §6.6 -- extends beyond the existing single-generation
// delete coverage (delete_template_incomplete_ok.gddl,
// delete_descendant_completes_and_resolves.gddl) to a THREE-generation chain:
// delete-marked template -> delete-marked template (copying the first) -> real,
// non-delete instance (copying the second). Isolates two things at once: (1) a
// `delete` instance copying from ANOTHER `delete` instance, carrying forward its
// incompleteness untouched; (2) confirms incompleteness tolerance survives multiple
// `delete` hops, and it is only the final non-`delete` instance that must fully
// resolve -- not "the first delete template plus one hop," but arbitrarily many hops.
//
// GRAMMAR CONFIRMED (previously flagged, now resolved and written into the spec):
// `TemplateGen2` is both a copy of `TemplateGen1` AND itself `delete`-marked, requiring
// the combined "copy-and-delete" instance header form. Confirmed correct as written:
// `Type Name = Source delete` (trailing `delete`, matching the single-keyword pattern
// from the plain `delete` example). No changes needed.

define Stats
	hp = u32
	mp = u32

Stats TemplateGen1 delete
	hp = 100
	// mp intentionally left uninitialized

Stats TemplateGen2 = TemplateGen1 delete
	// no new statements at all -- pure passthrough copy. Still `delete`, still
	// incomplete (mp remains untouched, inherited-uninitialized from TemplateGen1).

Stats RealFinal = TemplateGen2
	mp = 50
	// closes the gap left open across BOTH prior generations. RealFinal is not
	// `delete`, so it must fully resolve by export time -- and now it does.

// Expected (prediction, pending golden output from Compiler Core reference run):
//   Compiles cleanly, no error.
//   TemplateGen1, TemplateGen2: not present in exported output (both `delete`).
//   RealFinal.hp == 100 (carried across two generations, untouched the whole way)
//   RealFinal.mp == 50  (closes a gap left open since generation 1)
